WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.620
Devin Reeve:&nbsp; It’s interesting how projects
come to us.&nbsp;

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:06.450
My business partner (Larry Layfield) and I
have a company called InnoVate Productions.&nbsp;

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:15.340
We work as a creative development and producing
team, but we also work as a line producer

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:16.680
and a UPM team (Unit Production Manager).

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.830
So for this project, they reached out to us.&nbsp;

00:00:20.830 --> 00:00:26.820
They needed a line producer, someone to manage
this whole team for what we’re trying to

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.010
do.&nbsp;

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:33.800
And then other times people send us scripts
and we develop our own content and we produce

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:38.570
it and develop it, raise the funding and do
it ourselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:41.730
We have another film going on right now that
is actually filming, while we are filming

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:47.590
this (ROCK IN A HARD PLACE) that a friend
of mine was directing.&nbsp;

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:49.110
He had another producer on it.&nbsp;

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:59.060
He asked ‘Would you 
come in with your company and take over the

00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:00.060
production for me?”&nbsp;

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:03.430
And we were like “Absolutely,”&nbsp; so we
did that.&nbsp;

00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:05.999
It’s another reason why we’re tired.&nbsp;

00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:13.200
So we we’re hired, we partnered up with
people to create our own content and put things

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.719
out there.&nbsp;

00:01:14.719 --> 00:01:19.610
We do everything from music videos to commercials
to films.&nbsp;

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:27.550
We have a music video coming up with Holly
Holm who beat Ronda Rousey in February and

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:36.569
we’re actually doing a commercial with Ronda
(it was already set in place before she got

00:01:36.569 --> 00:01:43.159
beat) for an energy company that she has a
contract with.&nbsp;

00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:47.109
So lots of fun things coming our way.&nbsp;

00:01:47.109 --> 00:01:53.539
We developed a Halloween special that right
now is in development that is in a really

00:01:53.539 --> 00:02:00.149
good position to come out next year, so we’re
kind of moving forward, whether we create

00:02:00.149 --> 00:02:02.740
it yourself of people hire us, we just like
to keep working.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.489
Film Courage:&nbsp; Devin, you say you’ve been
doing this 15 years?

00:02:05.489 --> 00:02:10.119
Devin:&nbsp; Yeah, I’ve been acting since I
was a child (since I was 10) and I’ve been

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:16.370
producing since….first film I produced was
2005.&nbsp;

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:17.670
10 years.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:21.439
Film Courage:&nbsp; And so in the beginning, in
2005.&nbsp;

00:02:21.439 --> 00:02:25.569
Did you already have inroads through the acting
where you could reach out to people or was

00:02:25.569 --> 00:02:30.370
there a website or some place where you went
to look for people looking for line producers?

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:41.519
Devin:&nbsp; When I started, when I said “I
want to produce my own stuff,” very shortly

00:02:41.519 --> 00:02:45.860
after that I realized no one is just calling
you to produce.&nbsp;

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:48.800
No one’s calling to say, “Do you want
to produce a film with me?”&nbsp;

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.319
Because if they are, they’re calling you
to produce it because they need money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.319 --> 00:02:59.159
So part of a producer’s job is to raise
funding for it, if I’m the executive producers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.159 --> 00:03:01.120
So I taught myself to line produce.&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:07.780
I went and bought Movie Magic Budgeting.&nbsp;

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.969
I was already good with managing money and
managing people.&nbsp;

00:03:10.969 --> 00:03:15.450
I said “Oh, that’s not that bad.”&nbsp;

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:23.560
So the first thing I lined produced was a
short film, that was a $50,000 short film.&nbsp;

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:26.390
Pretty good budget for a short film.&nbsp;

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:33.930
Just started from there and then I think the
next thing I did was a feature film that was

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:42.160
like $200,000 and slowly just worked my way
up, so by making that transition, to line

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:47.609
producing it opened up other doors for people
to actually start calling,&nbsp; it opened up

00:03:47.609 --> 00:03:54.450
doors for producing and for acting, for line
producing and it was a good move for myself

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:58.230
because I was doing really well and I hit
a brick wall.&nbsp;

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:03.180
And I was like, “Oh man, what did my manager
say when I hit a certain age I hit a brick

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:07.249
wall and then after a couple years you will
kind of gain your momentum again” and she

00:04:07.249 --> 00:04:08.269
was just dead on.&nbsp;

00:04:08.269 --> 00:04:14.379
And that’s kind of what I did in my spare
time and now I’m lucky to be able to have

00:04:14.379 --> 00:04:16.910
those different skills to help you stay busy
all the time.

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:16.960
Question:&nbsp; Do you have any advice on finding
a movie producer?

